# Production build (Vercel). The backend is live at the URL below, so this build
# talks to it instead of mocking - the MSW-in-production shim that stood in for a
# missing backend is gone.
VITE_API_URL=https://amezo.onrender.com

# Real API, not the browser mock. Requires the backend endpoints the app calls to
# exist for real: GET /products (with category/price/stock filters and sort),
# GET /products/{id}, GET /variants?ids= (the cart's batch lookup), POST /orders,
# and the seller portal's own set. All present as of the catalog pass that came
# with this switch.
VITE_USE_MSW=false

# VITE_DEMO_SELLER_AUTH is gone, here and in the code. It skipped the magic-link
# round trip by minting a client-side session with no mp_session cookie behind it,
# which the whole app - the landing page included - then read as a signed-in
# seller. There is one way to sign in now, and it is the real one.
#
# Sign-in emails go out through Resend when RESEND_API_KEY is set on the backend
# (see docs/deploy.md); with no key the backend logs the link instead, and signing
# in means copying the token out of Render's log stream. Note Resend's sandbox only
# delivers to the Resend account's own address until a domain is verified, so the
# seeded demo@example.com seller cannot receive one yet.

# Cold start: the free Render instance sleeps after 15 minutes idle, so the first
# request after a quiet spell waits ~1 minute while it wakes, and can come back as
# a gateway error on the way. The frontend handles that rather than papering over
# it: a 20s deadline per attempt, bounded retries for infrastructure failures
# only, and a "waking the server up" banner instead of an error panel. See
# src/lib/api/transient.ts and docs/deploy.md.
